    "https://jobs.elon.edu/jobs/research-associate-in-insect-vibrational-communication-off-campus-north-carolina-united-states": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Insect Vibrational Communication",
      "description": "Research Associate in Insect Vibrational Communication - Off Campus, North Carolina, United States Research Associate in Insect Vibrational Communication - Off Campus, North Carolina, United States Kiosk mode Job Description for the Research Associate in Insect Vibrational Communication position. Review job description, requirements, and qualifications. Apply or register interest for the position. Research Associate in Insect Vibrational Communication Off Campus, North Carolina, 28213 Academic Affairs Staff Full-Time Title: Research Associate in Insect Vibrational Communication Position Type: Staff Full-Time Days Per Week: Monday - Friday Hours Per Week: 40 hrs/wk VP Area: Office of the Provost and Academic Affairs Department: Elon College, the College of Arts and Sciences Location: Off Campus Position Summary Elon University and the United States Department of Agriculture (USDA) are seeking a Research Associate with a background in animal communication to conduct exciting, cutting-edge research on the invasive spotted lanternfly (SLF) (Hemiptera: Lycorma delicatula). The ideal candidate would have expertise in the study of vibrational communication (i.e., biotremology) or acoustic communication. Research will focus on elucidating the role of SLF vibrational signals in insect mate selection behaviors, aggregation, and other behaviors that may be exploited as pest control methods. The successful candidate will work collaboratively with a team to develop vibrational signals into stimuli for novel behavior-based Integrated Pest Management tools and publish results in peer-reviewed journals. This position will be stationed at the USDA-APHIS Forest Pest Methods Laboratory (FPML) located in Buzzards Bay, Massachusetts, working closely with Dr. Miriam Cooperband at USDA-APHIS, in collaboration with Dr. Jennifer Hamel at Elon University and Dr. Rodrigo Krugner at USDA-ARS. Most work would occur at FPML, but the successful candidate would also travel to Elon University up to twice a year for field work and opportunities related to an academic setting. This position will support the training and development of a researcher with interests in insect communication and applied research. The successful candidate would work among a multidisciplinary team of scientists and technicians at the USDA APHIS PPQ FPML, gain experience investigating practical, real-world entomological problems involving important invasive species of insects, within a biosecure facility and in the field, and would also have opportunities to gain faculty- and student-facing experiences in an academic setting when visiting Elon University. Annual salary of $84,579 (Doctoral degree) or $69,905 (Master’s degree). This 12-month appointment has the possibility of renewal for up to three years. Benefits of Working at Elon Employees at Elon enjoy a generous and comprehensive benefits package that includes immediate eligibility for health, dental, and vision insurance, a retirement plan with an 8 percent contribution from the university, and 28 days of paid leave annually (vacation days and holidays). Elon values and celebrates the diverse backgrounds, cultures, experiences and perspectives of our community members. As an equal opportunity employer, Elon’s principles of diversity extend to race and gender identity, age, disability status, veteran status, sexual orientation, religion, and other aspects of one’s identity. At Elon, our employees respect human differences, passion for lifelong learning, emphasis on personal integrity and an ethic of service. Minimum Required Education and Experience Ph.D. or Master’s degree in Biology, Entomology, or a related discipline; demonstrated research experience and expertise in animal vibrational or acoustic communication. Applicants must be eligible to work in the U.S. under a cooperative agreement with the USDA and employment is contingent on passing a background check. Preferred Education and Experience Demonstrated expertise and success in vibrational communication research (i.e., biotremology); experience determining the behavioral function of vibrational signals; animal behavioral expertise relating specifically to substrate-borne vibrations used in aggregation, orientation, and/or mating; strong publication record. Job Duties Elon University and the United States Department of Agriculture (USDA) are seeking a Research Associate with a background in animal communication to conduct exciting, cutting-edge research on the invasive spotted lanternfly (SLF) (Hemiptera: Lycorma delicatula). The ideal candidate would have expertise in the study of vibrational communication (i.e., biotremology) or acoustic communication. Research will focus on elucidating the role of SLF vibrational signals in insect mate selection behaviors, aggregation, and other behaviors that may be exploited as pest control methods. The successful candidate will work collaboratively with a team to develop vibrational signals into stimuli for novel behavior-based Integrated Pest Management tools and publish results in peer-reviewed journals. This position will be stationed at the USDA-APHIS Forest Pest Methods Laboratory (FPML) located in Buzzards Bay, Massachusetts, working closely with Dr. Miriam Cooperband at USDA-APHIS, in collaboration with Dr. Jennifer Hamel at Elon University and Dr. Rodrigo Krugner at USDA-ARS. Most work would occur at FPML, but the successful candidate would also travel to Elon University up to twice a year for field work and opportunities related to an academic setting. This position will support the training and development of a researcher with interests in insect communication and applied research. The successful candidate would work among a multidisciplinary team of scientists and technicians at the USDA APHIS PPQ FPML, gain experience investigating practical, real-world entomological problems involving important invasive species of insects, within a biosecure facility and in the field, and would also have opportunities to gain faculty- and student-facing experiences in an academic setting when visiting Elon University. Annual salary of $84,579 (Doctoral degree) or $69,905 (Master’s degree). This 12-month appointment has the possibility of renewal for up to three years. Special Instructions to Applicants: First deadline to apply is October 18, 2026, but the position will remain open until filled. Apply now! Sign up for job alerts! Job Alert f7b7c8ac Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments Elon College, the College of Arts and Sciences Elon College, the College of Arts and Sciences Locations Off Campus, North Carolina, United States Off Campus, North Carolina, United States First Name (required) 1e5bdf21 Last Name (required) 57d673b3 Email (required) bf2fc491 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs Regional Assistant Director of Admissions- Texas Admissions The regional assistant director of admissions is responsible for all aspects of undergraduate recruitment for students from Texas. In addition, this role will also provide territory management for Oklahoma and Louisiana. The regional assistant director gives university information to prospective students and families through various communication methods. The regional assistant director is also responsible for territory development, along with building and maintaining strong relationships with high schools, counselors, and being active in relevant regional associations. The position is an 11-month regional position, and the ideal candidate will reside within 30 miles of Dallas, Austin or Houston metro regions. Regional Assistant Director of Admissions- Florida Admissions The regional assistant director of admissions is responsible for all aspects of undergraduate recruitment for students from Florida. In addition, this role will also provide territory management for Mississippi, Arkansas and Puerto Rico. The regional assistant director gives university information to prospective students and families through various communication methods. The regional assistant director is also responsible for territory development, along with building and maintaining strong relationships with high schools, counselors, and being active in relevant regional associations. The position is an 11-month regional position, and the ideal candidate will reside within 30 miles of Miami, Tampa or Orlando metro regions. Construction Project Manager Planning, Design & Construction Mgmt. The role leads projects through construction and closeout, with particular emphasis on contractor performance, schedule management, cost control, quality, and timely resolution of construction issues. The Construction Project Manager proactively identifies risks and potential delays, coordinates timely solutions to maintain project milestones and support successful completion, and monitors contractor and design team performance against contractual commitments. The position also provides construction expertise during planning and design to improve constructability, phasing, procurement, and schedule performance. Instructional Technologist – Digital Learning and Digital Accessibility Information Technology The Instructional Technologist – Digital Learning and Digital Accessibility supports the Hayworth Center for Digital Learning (HCDL) as part of Teaching and Learning Technologies (TLT), providing faculty- and staff-facing expertise in digital learning technologies, digital accessibility, and learning design. The position provides consultative support for Microsoft 365, Canvas (LMS), and other digital learning tools, helping faculty and staff use technology effectively to support teaching, learning, collaboration, and university operations. The position also advances digital acce